<!--
Blitzy skin verification fixture. harness/provision.sh authors this page with
core's maintenance/edit.php, which reads the content from standard input:

    php maintenance/edit.php 'Main Page' \
        --user "$BLITZY_TEST_USER" \
        --summary 'Blitzy fixture: seed revision for the history and diff captures' \
        < skins/Blitzy/fixtures/history-seed.wikitext

The provisioning contract, recorded here so that it travels with the file:

* The target title is exactly "Main Page", and this revision is saved directly
  before fixtures/main-page.wikitext. Reversing the two would leave this draft
  as the current revision, and the lavender band and the card grid that belong
  on the main page would vanish from its captures.
* Do NOT pass --createonly. MediaWiki's installer has already created Main Page
  (includes/Installer/Task/InitialContentTask.php), so edit.php would stop with
  "Page already exists" instead of saving.
* Never pass --bot or --no-rc, here or on any other authored fixture edit. They
  set EDIT_FORCE_BOT and EDIT_SUPPRESS_RC, and a bot edit is hidden from the
  default Special:RecentChanges view while a suppressed one never reaches it at
  all. Special:RecentChanges is itself a captured surface, so either flag would
  empty the very list that capture exists to show. --minor is unnecessary as
  well: leave it off so that both authored history rows read the same way.
* Saving this pair is not idempotent. A second provisioning run appends another
  seed revision and another main page revision, which lengthens the history and
  changes the history capture, so provision.sh must either start from a fresh
  database volume or skip this step when Main Page already holds more than the
  installer's single revision. Re-saving the talk page and the category page is
  a null edit that stores no revision, so only this pair needs the guard.
* No capture URL may hardcode a revision id. Imported revisions do not keep the
  ids recorded in the XML (see ImportableOldRevisionImporter), and the ids of
  these two edits are assigned when they are saved, so the diff URL has to be
  resolved at capture time from the history page or the API, and the resolved
  URL recorded in DELIVERY.md.
* --user must be BLITZY_TEST_USER, the ordinary preferences-populated account,
  and never MW_ADMIN_USER, because no capture may display the administrator
  name. The installer's own revision belongs to the system user
  "MediaWiki default", so the page history stays clear of the administrator too.
* The markup below is fixed on purpose: no signature tildes, nothing the
  pre-save transform can expand, and no time-varying magic word. edit.php saves
  through PageUpdater, and DerivedPageDataUpdater::prepareContent() applies
  preSaveTransform, so anything expandable would be rewritten at provision time
  and consecutive capture runs would stop being byte-identical.
-->
This wiki is the dedicated verification instance for the '''Blitzy''' skin. It holds a small,
unchanging set of reference articles imported from Wikipedia, together with the talk, category
and history pages needed to exercise every surface the skin styles. Nothing on it is generated
at view time, so each page renders the same way on every run, and ordinary MediaWiki behaviour
is left untouched: [[Special:RecentChanges|recent changes]], page histories, diffs, searching
and the edit form all work exactly as they do under any other skin.

== Reference articles ==

Four articles are imported together with their templates, so that every content treatment the
skin styles has a real instance to render.

Between them they cover the content treatments the skin has to style: an infobox, a wikitable,
a code sample, a hatnote, a reference list and a deep heading tree.

* [[Ada Lovelace]] - a biography, carrying a floated infobox, images and a dense reference list.
* [[Python (programming language)]] - a software article, carrying an infobox, syntax-highlighted code samples and nested lists.
* [[List of countries by GDP (nominal)]] - a large sortable wikitable, and the widest layout on the wiki: the horizontal overflow test.
* [[Photosynthesis]] - deep heading nesting, chemical notation, diagrams and hatnotes.

